                  "code": "67781-5",
                  "system": "http://loinc.org",
                  "display": "Summarization of encounter note"
                }
              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the hospital course of your 57-year-old female patient, who was admitted on 31 December 1997 with acute lower abdominal pain and suspected tubal pregnancy. On admission, physical examination and transvaginal ultrasonography confirmed an ectopic tubal pregnancy with signs of early rupture. Serum beta-hCG was markedly elevated. Given the patient's hemodynamic stability and the sonographic findings, the decision was made for surgical intervention. She underwent laparoscopic salpingectomy for tubal ectopic pregnancy, which was performed without complications. Intravenous fluid replacement was administered perioperatively. Postoperative recovery was uneventful, and the patient was discharged on 05 January 1998 in good general condition. We recommend follow-up of beta-hCG levels and gynaecological review within two weeks. Please do not hesitate to contact us should any questions arise.</div>"
            },
            "code": {
